 ],
 "literal": "Deer's hoof like cleft-leaved adumbu's / harness-bell like bright flowers plucking, / bright-bangle-wearing girls play with sand-castles, / on the bird-resounding great sea's shore-lord, / I will not think of, my friend, so that my eyes may sleep!",
 "translation": "I will not think of him, my friend—the lord of the great sea-shore where birds chirp, and where girls wearing bright bangles pluck the bright flowers, shaped like harness bells, of the cleft-leaved adumbu creeper that resembles a deer's hoof, to play in their sand-castles. I will not think of him, so that my eyes may finally close in sleep.",
 "modern": "மான் குளம்பு போன்ற பிளந்த இலைகளைக் கொண்ட அடும்புக் கொடியின், குதிரை மாலையின் மணி போன்ற ஒளிரும் பூக்களைக் கொய்து, ஒளிரும் வளையல்களை அணிந்த பெண்கள் வண்டல் விளையாட்டு விளையாடும், பறவைகள் ஒலிக்கும் பெரிய கடற்கரைத் தலைவனை, தோழியே! நான் நினைக்க மாட்டேன்; என் கண்கள் உறங்கட்டும்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend in a state of deep distress caused by the hero's absence. She is unable to sleep because her mind is constantly filled with thoughts of him. In her frustration, she declares that she will stop thinking about the lord of the shore so that her eyes can finally find rest. This poem belongs to the Neytal (maritime) landscape, characterized by coastal elements like the adumbu creeper, chirping sea-birds, and girls playing in the sand. The imagery of the girls plucking bell-like flowers to play happily contrasts with the heroine's sleepless grief. Key words include adumbu (the coastal creeper), vaṇṭal (sandplay), and uḷḷēṉ (I will not think of).",
